\description{
Estimates impulse response functions (IRFs) using recursive zero
restrictions combined with local projections (Jorda, 2005). The shock of
dimension \code{e} is identified by regressing the outcome at horizon \code{h} by OLS
on the contemporaneous value of the \code{e}-th variable in \code{y}, controlling for
the contemporaneous values of variables \verb{1, ..., e - 1} in \code{y}, lags of the
information set, and deterministic terms. This is the local-projection
analogue of a Cholesky decomposition and serves as a benchmark for the
heteroskedasticity-based estimator \code{\link[=hetiv]{hetiv()}}.
}
\details{
For \code{E > 1}, identification is recursive and order-dependent: the
column order of \code{y} defines both the shock ordering and the normalization
variable for each shock dimension. By construction, the impact response
of variable \code{e} to shock \code{e} equals \code{norm}, and the impact responses of
variables \verb{1, ..., e - 1} to shock \code{e} are zero.

Unlike \code{\link[=hetiv]{hetiv()}}, the distinction between policy days (\code{Ind == 1}) and
control days (\code{Ind == 0}) does not affect the IRF estimates: both enter
the local projections, and only contaminated days (\code{Ind == 2}) are
dropped. The distinction matters only for the shock extraction returned
when \code{details = TRUE}.
}
